{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":179837,"duration_api_ms":179388,"num_turns":7,"result":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the codebase. Let me produce the planning payload.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"problem_framing\": \"The tank simulator has an internal unit taxonomy that evolved organically, creating three distinct problem areas that need a written policy before refactors begin:\\n\\n1. **Storage-vs-display semantic mismatch**: WaterState stores nitrogen pools as whole-tank elemental totals (e.g., `ammonia_total_mg_n_total` in mg N total), but TankSnapshot exposes fields like `nitrite_mg_l` and `nitrate_mg_l` whose names read like ion-mass concentrations (NO2⁻ mg/L, NO3⁻ mg/L) when they are actually elemental-N concentrations (mg N/L). This has already been flagged in the scientific inventory (§1.6) as a misread risk. Similarly, `phosphate_mg_l` is mg P/L, not mg PO₄/L, and `nh3_mg_l` is mg N/L as free ammonia-N, not molecular NH₃ mg/L.\\n\\n2. **TDS and conductivity labeling**: `tds_mg_l` sums only 7 tracked ions (Ca, Mg, Na, K, HCO₃⁻, Cl⁻, SO₄²⁻) and `conductivity_us_cm` is derived via a fixed `tds / 0.65` divisor. Both fields present as authoritative measurements but are rough estimates. The policy must label these clearly so API consumers and TUI users understand the approximation.\\n\\n3. **Type safety strategy**: All quantities are bare `f64` with unit information encoded only in field names. The project needs an explicit decision on whether to introduce newtypes (e.g., `MgNTotal(f64)`, `MgNPerL(f64)`, `MgIonPerL(f64)`) or rely on naming conventions, and where the boundary lies.\\n\\nThis policy document must be written and approved before any refactoring beads (tanksim-6e5.2.2, tanksim-6e5.2.7, tanksim-6e5.4.1, tanksim-6e5.5.1) can proceed, as all of them depend on consistent unit semantics.\",\n\n  \"assumptions_or_open_questions\": [\n    \"Decision 1: Keep internal storage as mg-element-total (current pattern) or switch to mg-element-per-L? Recommendation: keep totals internally — avoids recomputing volume on every read, provides concentration via helpers. Source water profiles already use per-L and multiply by volume on materialization, validating this pattern.\",\n    \"Decision 2: Display units — show mg N/L (scientific, EPA/test-kit compatible for TAN) or convert to mg-ion/L (hobby-friendly, matches API test kit labels like NO₃⁻ mg/L)? Recommendation: offer both with clear labeling. Conversion factors: NO₃⁻ ion = N × 62/14 ≈ 4.43×; NO₂⁻ ion = N × 46/14 ≈ 3.29×; NH₄⁺ ion = N × 18/14 ≈ 1.29×; PO₄³⁻ = P × 95/31 ≈ 3.06×.\",\n    \"Decision 3: Type safety — full newtypes (MgNTotal, MgNPerL, MgIonPerL) or naming convention with selective newtypes? Recommendation: start with strict naming convention enforced by review, plus a small set of newtypes for the highest-risk conversion boundaries (total ↔ concentration, element ↔ ion). Defer a full newtype forest until the API stabilizes.\",\n    \"Open question: Should the API default to scientific (mg N/L) or hobby (mg ion/L) display, and should it offer a query parameter or separate endpoints for the alternative? This affects tanksim-6e5.2.7.\",\n    \"Open question: Should save files continue storing raw totals (current behavior) or also embed the volume_l used for materialization so consumers can derive concentrations without recomputing geometry? This affects data file round-tripping.\",\n    \"Assumption: The half-saturation parameters in ProcessParams that currently use whole-tank totals (aob_k_tan_mg, nob_k_nitrite_mg, etc.) will be converted to concentration-based values by tanksim-6e5.2.2, not by this policy bead. This bead only documents the target naming convention for those fields.\",\n    \"Assumption: TDS and conductivity will remain estimates for the foreseeable future. The policy should label them as 'estimated from tracked ions' rather than promising full implementation.\"\n  ],\n\n  \"proposed_work\": [\n    {\n      \"order\": 0,\n      \"summary\": \"Draft the internal storage convention section of UNITS.md\",\n      \"details\": \"Create `docs/UNITS.md` with a section covering internal storage rules:\\n\\n- **Rule 1 — Totals for conserved pools**: All dissolved and particulate pools in `WaterState`, `SubstrateLayerState`, and `DetritusBiology` store absolute whole-tank totals. Field names MUST include a `_total` suffix (e.g., `ammonia_total_mg_n_total`, `calcium_mg_total`). The element basis MUST appear in the name: `_mg_n_` for nitrogen-as-element, `_mg_p_` for phosphorus-as-element, `_mg_c_` for carbon-as-element, `_mg_` (bare) for species where the element IS the ion (Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻) or molecular mass (O₂, SO₄²⁻, HCO₃⁻).\\n- **Rule 2 — Non-total exceptions**: `temperature_c`, `ph`, and 0..1 index fields are not totals and MUST NOT carry `_total`. pH is a derived field re-computed each step.\\n- **Rule 3 — Source water profiles**: Per-liter inputs use `_per_l` suffix (e.g., `ammonia_mg_n_per_l`). Materialization multiplies by `geometry.water_volume_l()` to produce totals.\\n- **Rule 4 — Parameters**: Rate parameters use explicit unit suffixes (`_per_hour`, `_per_day`, `_per_g_per_hour`). Half-saturation constants that are currently in whole-tank totals (`_mg` suffix) MUST be migrated to concentration form (`_mg_per_l` suffix) by tanksim-6e5.2.2.\\n\\nInclude a table mapping each current WaterState field to its canonical name under the convention (most already comply; flag any that don't).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"order\": 1,\n      \"summary\": \"Draft the concentration helper naming convention section\",\n      \"details\": \"Add a section to `docs/UNITS.md` covering helper method naming:\\n\\n- **Concentration helpers**: Methods that divide a total by volume MUST be named `<pool>_mg_<element>_per_l(&self, volume_l: f64) -> f64`. Example: `ammonia_mg_n_per_l(volume_l)`, `nitrite_mg_n_per_l(volume_l)`. These live on `WaterState` (or a future `WaterConcentrations` view struct).\\n- **Ion-display helpers**: Methods that convert element-basis concentration to ion-mass concentration MUST be named `<pool>_mg_<ion>_per_l(mg_element_per_l: f64) -> f64`. Example: `nitrate_mg_no3_per_l(mg_n_per_l)`, `nitrite_mg_no2_per_l(mg_n_per_l)`. These are pure functions (no state), grouped in a `unit_conversions` module.\\n- **Snapshot field naming**: Snapshot fields that show element-basis concentrations MUST include the element in the name: `tan_mg_n_per_l`, `nitrite_mg_n_per_l`, `nitrate_mg_n_per_l`, `phosphate_mg_p_per_l`. The current names (`nitrite_mg_l`, `nitrate_mg_l`) violate this and will be renamed by tangsim-6e5.2.7.\\n- Document the conversion constants in one canonical location: NO₃⁻/N = 62/14, NO₂⁻/N = 46/14, NH₄⁺/N = 18/14, PO₄³⁻/P = 95/31.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"order\": 2,\n      \"summary\": \"Draft the display and labeling policy section\",\n      \"details\": \"Add a section to `docs/UNITS.md` covering display rules for TUI, API, and data files:\\n\\n- **Default display**: The API and TUI default to scientific element-basis concentrations (mg N/L for nitrogen species, mg P/L for phosphorus). This matches EPA reporting convention and is unambiguous.\\n- **Hobby display mode**: A secondary display mode converts to ion-mass concentrations (mg NO₃⁻/L, mg NO₂⁻/L, mg NH₄⁺/L, mg PO₄³⁻/L) for users accustomed to hobby test kits (API, Salifert, etc.). The TUI offers a toggle; the API accepts a `unit_style=ion` query parameter.\\n- **Labeling requirement**: Every displayed concentration MUST include an unambiguous unit label. Element-basis: 'mg N/L' or 'mg-N/L'. Ion-basis: 'mg NO₃⁻/L'. Never bare 'mg/L' for nitrogen or phosphorus species.\\n- **TDS/conductivity labels**: TDS MUST be labeled 'Est. TDS (7-ion)' or similar, with a tooltip/footnote listing which ions are summed. Conductivity MUST be labeled 'Est. conductivity' with the `÷0.65` method noted. Neither field should be presented as a measurement.\\n- **GH/KH labels**: Label as 'GH (Ca+Mg)' and 'KH (alkalinity)' to convey the derivation basis.\\n- **Precision policy**: Define standard decimal places per quantity (pH: 1-2, nitrogen: 2-3, GH/KH: 1, TDS: 0, conductivity: 0).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"order\": 3,\n      \"summary\": \"Draft the newtype strategy section\",\n      \"details\": \"Add a section to `docs/UNITS.md` covering the type safety roadmap:\\n\\n- **Phase 1 (naming convention only)**: Enforce the naming convention from orders 0-1 through code review and clippy lints where possible. No newtypes introduced yet. This is the immediate scope.\\n- **Phase 2 (targeted newtypes for high-risk boundaries)**: Introduce newtypes at the two highest-risk conversion boundaries:\\n  1. `MgElementTotal(f64)` vs `MgElementPerL(f64)` — prevents accidentally using a total where a concentration is expected (the most common bug vector given snapshot fields are concentrations but kinetics currently use totals).\\n  2. `MgElementPerL(f64)` vs `MgIonPerL(f64)` — prevents silently displaying element-basis values with ion-basis labels.\\n  These newtypes live in a `crates/tank_core/src/types/units.rs` module and implement `From`/`Into` only through explicit conversion methods that require volume_l or a conversion factor.\\n- **Phase 3 (deferred)**: Broader newtype coverage (e.g., `DegreesC`, `MeqTotal`, `WPerM2K`) evaluated after API stabilization. Explicitly defer to avoid premature abstraction.\\n- Document the rationale: a smaller number of well-named helpers beats a forest of wrapper types if the API is disciplined. The newtypes exist to catch the mistakes that naming alone cannot prevent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"order\": 4,\n      \"summary\": \"Review policy against all dependent beads and validate completeness\",\n      \"details\": \"Cross-reference the draft UNITS.md against the acceptance criteria and downstream dependents:\\n\\n- Verify that field names and helper names have a consistent convention (AC #1) by checking every WaterState, TankSnapshot, and ProcessParams field against the rules.\\n- Verify the display policy is written down before refactors start (AC #2).\\n- Verify the strategy is explicit enough to prevent semantic drift in future data files (AC #3) — specifically that source water TOML field names, save-file field names, and snapshot/API field names all follow a single coherent scheme.\\n- Check that tanksim-6e5.2.2 (concentration helpers), tanksim-6e5.2.7 (snapshot/API renaming), tanksim-6e5.4.1 (carbonate contract), and tanksim-6e5.5.1 (habitat registry) can each cite UNITS.md for naming decisions without ambiguity.\\n- Flag any fields that need renaming and list them as explicit migration items for downstream beads.\"\n    }\n  ],\n\n  \"readiness\": {\n    \"ready\": true,\n    \"risks\": [\n      \"API breaking change: renaming snapshot fields (nitrite_mg_l → nitrite_mg_n_per_l, etc.) will break any external consumers. The policy should define a migration/deprecation strategy even if no external consumers exist today, since save files serialize snapshot data.\",\n      \"Save file migration: existing save files contain the current field names. Serde aliases or a version-bump migration will be needed. The policy document should acknowledge this but the implementation belongs to tanksim-6e5.2.7.\",\n      \"Scope creep risk: the newtype strategy could expand beyond the targeted Phase 2 if contributors over-apply it. The policy must be explicit about what is deferred to Phase 3.\",\n      \"Dual-display complexity: offering both element-basis and ion-basis display modes doubles the TUI/API surface for chemistry values. The policy should recommend a single default with opt-in for the alternative to limit complexity.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"7e228030-7c5e-4705-b5bc-f59d5a5cb812","total_cost_usd":0.6423118499999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":46604,"cache_read_input_tokens":128696,"output_tokens":4316,"server_tool_use"